---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import RenderLicenceIllustration from '../../components/RenderLicenceIllustration.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'render')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Registry Render generates and signs your service’s documents."
  description="It creates PDF licences, certificates, letters and receipts from your service’s data and approved templates. It can add your institution’s digital seal with your institution’s signing key. It adds a timestamp when you configure one."
  note="Open-source software to generate and sign documents in a new service or one you already run."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="render-example-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Example: a professional licence</p>
        <h2 id="render-example-title">Create a licence document that carries your institution’s seal.</h2>
        <p>The authority grants a licence. The service then sends the holder’s details and the licence terms to Render. Render generates and signs the document. The service stores it and makes it available to the holder.</p>
      </div>
      <RenderLicenceIllustration />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="render-capabilities-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What Render does</p>
        <h2 id="render-capabilities-title">Prepare each document the same way, then sign it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Use approved templates</h3><p>Define the layout, labels, fonts and supported languages. Package the reviewed template so your service uses a known version.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Check the required information</h3><p>Set the data each document needs. Render checks the supplied data against that definition before it generates the PDF.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Sign with your institution’s key</h3><p>Apply a digital seal to the PDF. Recipients can validate the signature against your institution’s certificate. They can also check whether the signed content has changed. You can add a timestamp from the authority you configure. This is your institution’s seal. It is not a personal e-signature. It does not promise qualified legal status.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="fit" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="render-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your service</p>
        <h2 id="render-fit-title">Generate and sign documents beside your register’s records.</h2>
        <p>Your application can request a generated and signed document from Render’s service. For generation alone, use a local command or embed Render in your software. The data may come from <a href="/products/base-registry/">Base Registry Engine</a>, which keeps your register’s records. It may also come from another system.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your institution defines the document and the seal</dt><dd>Agree the content and the template. Agree when the service may issue the document. For signing, configure the certificate and the applications that may request a seal. Also configure a key held in Vault or OpenBao Transit. Timestamping uses the authority you choose.</dd>
        <dt>Your application keeps and shares the result</dt><dd>The implementation stores the PDF and its identifying hashes. It also provides the download, printing or document access route that people need.</dd>
        <dt>Choose who runs it</dt><dd>Your own team or a provider you appoint can host and operate Render. Or we run it for you. We quote hosting and operation separately in the proposal.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="render-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evaluate the software</p>
        <h2 id="render-developers-title">Generate your first document.</h2>
        <p>The tutorial generates a local letter. It then requests a PDF through the service interface. The <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/start/registry-render/">Render overview</a> explains the product. The <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/operate/registry-render/">operations guide</a> covers running it. The <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/operate/registry-render/#seal-documents">signing setup</a> shows how to apply a PAdES digital seal with or without a timestamp. PAdES is a standard for signing PDF files. The guide includes a test seal to evaluate locally. It also sets out the requirements for your institution’s signing key.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>{product.tutorialLabel} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>
